    description: 'A novelist investigates why his lover ended their affair, discovering that her promise to God may have been the cause.',
    coverColor: C[3], coverAccent: A[4], isbn: '978-0-14-243730-8', publicDomain: false, classic: true,
    reviewCommentary: 'The End of the Affair is Greene\'s most personal novel, based on his own affair with Catherine Walston. Bendrix, the novelist whose jealousy leads him to investigate God, is one of literature\'s most complex narrators. Sarah, the woman whose promise to end the affair if God saves Bendrix\'s life becomes a vow she cannot break, is one of Greene\'s most sympathetic heroines. The novel\'s exploration of the relationship between love, jealousy, and faith is the most sustained in Catholic literature.',
    whyRead: 'It is the most passionate of Greene\'s Catholic novels, a love story that becomes a story about God. It is also the most autobiographical, and its emotional honesty is devastating.',
